### What is the Top K Frequent Elements problem?

Given an array of elements, return the k elements that appear most frequently. For example, in `[1,1,1,2,2,3]` with k=2, the answer is `[1, 2]`.

At the National Bank of India, the operations dashboard displays the k most frequent transaction types each day.

### How does the frequency counting phase work?

A single pass builds a HashMap from element to its count.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FrequencyMap {
    public static Map<String, Integer> countFrequencies(String[] transactions) {
        Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (String txn : transactions) {
            freq.merge(txn, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        return freq;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] txns = {"UPI", "NEFT", "UPI", "IMPS", "UPI", "NEFT"};
        System.out.println(countFrequencies(txns));
    }
}
```

```text
{UPI=3, NEFT=2, IMPS=1}
```

### How does the min-heap selection work?

Maintain a min-heap of size k ordered by frequency. Offer each distinct element; if the heap exceeds k, poll the least frequent.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.*;

public class TopKFrequent {
    public static List<String> topKFrequent(String[] transactions, int k) {
        Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (String txn : transactions) {
            freq.merge(txn, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        PriorityQueue<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> heap =
                new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Map.Entry::getValue));

        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            heap.offer(entry);
            if (heap.size() > k) {
                heap.poll();
            }
        }

        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            result.add(heap.poll().getKey());
        }
        Collections.reverse(result);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] txns = {"UPI", "NEFT", "UPI", "IMPS", "UPI", "NEFT", "RTGS"};
        System.out.println("Top 2: " + topKFrequent(txns, 2));
    }
}
```

```text
Top 2: [UPI, NEFT]
```

### How does the bucket sort alternative work?

Since any count is between 1 and n, create n+1 buckets indexed by frequency. Drop each element into the bucket matching its count. Scan from the highest bucket down, collecting k elements.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.*;

public class TopKBucketSort {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static List<String> topKFrequent(String[] transactions, int k) {
        Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (String txn : transactions) {
            freq.merge(txn, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        List<String>[] buckets = new List[transactions.length + 1];
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            int count = entry.getValue();
            if (buckets[count] == null) {
                buckets[count] = new ArrayList<>();
            }
            buckets[count].add(entry.getKey());
        }

        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = buckets.length - 1; i >= 0 && result.size() < k; i--) {
            if (buckets[i] != null) {
                result.addAll(buckets[i]);
            }
        }
        return result.subList(0, k);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] txns = {"UPI", "NEFT", "UPI", "IMPS", "UPI", "NEFT", "RTGS"};
        System.out.println("Top 2 (bucket): " + topKFrequent(txns, 2));
    }
}
```

```text
Top 2 (bucket): [UPI, NEFT]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Approach | Counting | Selection | Total |
|---|---|---|---|
| Min-heap | O(n) | O(m log k) | O(n + m log k) |
| Bucket sort | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |
| Full sort | O(n) | O(m log m) | O(n + m log m) |

The bucket sort approach is linear because it exploits the fact that frequencies are bounded by n.

### How does the heap comparison work?

The heap is a min-heap by frequency, so the root always holds the least frequent of the current leaders. When a new entry arrives with a higher frequency than the root, the root is evicted and replaced.

```java
// Min-heap: smallest frequency at root
PriorityQueue<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> heap =
    new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Map.Entry::getValue));

// After processing: heap contains exactly k entries
// Root = least frequent of the top k
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using a max-heap instead of a min-heap. A max-heap would keep evicting the most frequent element, which is the opposite of what you want.

```java
// Wrong: max-heap evicts the most frequent
new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(e -> -e.getValue()));

// Correct: min-heap evicts the least frequent
new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Map.Entry::getValue));
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **k equals the number of distinct elements**: return all distinct elements.
- **All elements identical**: the single element is the only answer for k=1.
- **Multiple elements share the same frequency as the kth**: the problem says "any order," so ties can be broken arbitrarily.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The insight interviewers reward is recognizing that frequencies are bounded by n, which is exactly what makes bucket sort linear. Both approaches share the identical frequency-map phase; they differ only in selection.

For streaming data where you cannot wait for the full array, use the heap approach because it processes elements one at a time. For batch computation where latency matters, use bucket sort.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What if k is very large, close to the number of distinct elements? (Bucket sort is better.)
- What if elements arrive in a stream? (Use heap with the frequency map updated on each arrival.)
- How would you handle ties? (Problem usually says any order; if not, add a secondary sort key.)
